[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi! I just opened a small mathlib PR:<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42110\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42110</a></p>\n<p>It adds <code>Path.length</code> for paths in a <code>PseudoEMetricSpace</code>, defined via variation, together with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Path.edist_le_length</code></li>\n<li><code>Path.length_refl</code></li>\n<li><code>Path.length_symm</code></li>\n<li><code>Path.length_trans</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is intended as a first step toward a broader theory of rectifiable paths.<br>\nI’d be very happy for any comments or review suggestions. Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 612816852,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1785068656
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Eventually I will prove that in a strict convex space, every shortest path between two points is the straight line segment up to monotonic reparametrisation.</p>",
        "id": 612816928,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1785068772
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>OOps, one simp fail in checks, it was fine on my computer. I will correct it later.</p>",
        "id": 612817731,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1785069740
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok it is fixed now. Hi everyone, welcome to review it!</p>",
        "id": 612829994,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1785081563
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think this overlaps with the path_metric.lean file in this old <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/pull/18375/changes#diff-18cc727c27203e8b90e66abc2f9f7b116a8125e4f1592c8b861b1c04dc3de7da\">mathlib3 PR</a>. The proofs there might inspire you to golf your proofs. Part of that PR is ported to <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31766\">#31766</a>, but not the path_metric file, and some review comments remain to be addressed. You might consider how to best integrate your PR with <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31766\">#31766</a>.</p>",
        "id": 612850931,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1785094415
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks, this is very helpful. I took a closer look at <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31766\">#31766</a>, and I really like it — it is clearly more general, and in many ways it feels like the better long-term design for mathlib. In particular, I like that many of the early results are developed in a more general setting rather than only for paths.</p>\n<p>My hesitation is mainly practical: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31766\">#31766</a> is quite old (from 2023), and if I am reading the history correctly, the last commit there was in December 2025. So I am a bit worried that if I tie this small PR too closely to that development, it may get stuck for a long time as well.</p>\n<p>Given that, what do you think is the best thing for me to do here? Would you recommend that I keep this PR as a small independent path-specific result, or would it be better to close it and instead try to contribute in the direction of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31766\">#31766</a>? I’d be very happy to follow whichever direction seems more appropriate.</p>",
        "id": 612863871,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1785108147
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Probably off-topic, but this inspired me to look into the definition of path length, as I have some recency bias on Hausdorff measure and that is what popped up in my mind first for path length. I ended up having Aristotle to verify the following lemma</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Topology</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BoundedVariation</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">MeasureTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Hausdorff</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ENNReal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasureTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">BorelSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- eVariation and Hausdorff measure are mostly equivalent for path length</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eVariationOn_eq_hausdorffMeasure_of_ordConnected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">OrdConnected</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hinj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">InjOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hcont</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ContinuousOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">eVariationOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μH</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">''</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- It is relatively short to show the shortest paths are line segments under Hausdorff measure</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- since you mentioned in the PR that this is on your road map</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">edist_le_hausdorffMeasure_one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsPreconnected</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hy</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">edist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μH</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I think your definition using <code>eVariationOn</code> is still the more correct one for non-injective path. I might formalize some results about Hausdorff measure independently, but please let me know if you happen to already touch those as well</p>",
        "id": 615505885,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1786315084
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Nope. My ultimate goal is the following code, which says  between any two points in a strictly convex normed space, a shortest path exists, and every shortest path is the straight segment up to monotone reparametrization.<br>\nThe definition of path length in my mind comes from this lecture notes of Terry tao on  <a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2016/09/27/246a-notes-2-complex-integration/\">Complex analysis</a> , see Def 6. And this concept is called arc length of a rectifiable curve. Eventually I should be able to link it up with integration for some conditions that are as weak as possible. But recently I don't want to touch that, because the code would be too long to review and I am busy with another project concerning local field and global field. So you can do it.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- Monotone self-maps of the unit interval fixing the endpoints. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonotoneReparam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- The underlying self-map of the unit interval. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">I</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A monotone reparametrization is continuous. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A monotone reparametrization fixes the left endpoint. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">map_zero'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A monotone reparametrization fixes the right endpoint. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">map_one'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"sd\">/-- A monotone reparametrization is monotone. -/</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">monotone_toFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Monotone</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">toFun</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">StrictConvexSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Between any two points in a strictly convex normed space, a shortest path exists, and every</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">shortest path is the straight segment up to monotone reparametrization. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exists_shortestPath_unique_up_to_reparamMonotone</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonotoneReparam</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">segment</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">reparamMonotone</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨?_</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">segment</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">segment_is_shortest</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">τ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hγ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">shortestPath_eq_segment_reparam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">γ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hγ</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 615516869,
        "sender_full_name": "Zhenhua Wu",
        "timestamp": 1786321387
    }
]